guide-entry-nc-cyberarms = Cyberarms: assembly and service
nc-guide-arms-1 = [bold]A limb with replaceable modules[/bold]
nc-guide-arms-2 = Install a cyberarm with the existing graft surgery. Every new cyberarm includes a mechanical hand, grafted together with the arm. Functional attachments are fitted separately. A powered implant bus and final neural calibration are required. Civilian and armored frames add a mounting cuff and shell to the existing actuator, controller and sensor assembly.
nc-guide-arms-3 = Print an empty frame and its parts. Fit them on a cyberware bench, use the standard firmware card, test with a charged bench battery and prepare modules with sterile wrapping. Parts have four quality grades. A power drive replaces the normal actuator. Ammunition is separate.
nc-guide-arms-4 = Each arm has one hand mount, one wrist socket and three forearm bays, sharing four units of volume. Impact hands, claws, tools, medical tools and pockets use one; blades, monowire and pistols use two; mantis blades use three; launchers use four. A launcher also needs a heavy arm with a power drive.
nc-guide-arms-5 = Hold a service probe, open the wearer’s context menu and open the arm panel. Hold the prepared module and choose a bay. Calibrate with the probe and close the panel. Open/close takes two seconds; installation, removal and calibration take four. Movement or damage interrupts service.
nc-guide-arms-6 = A calibrated quick-release wrist permits hand attachments to be removed, fitted and calibrated without opening the panel, one second per step. Forearm modules still require panel access. An intact detached cassette keeps its factory preparation but loses wearer calibration.
nc-guide-arms-7 = The side’s hand must be free to deploy a tool or weapon. Wait for the mechanism to extend. Reload the pistol or launcher with ordinary ammunition; retracting preserves its contents. The hidden pocket holds Small items in a 2×2 grid. The surgical hand supports incision, hemostasis, retraction and suturing; it cannot be left in a wound as a loose clamp.

